    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "Does cold weather raise blood sugar directly?",
        "a": "Mostly no, on the evidence available. Average glucose does run higher in winter in temperate countries, but the pattern looks like a response to the season rather than to the temperature. The clearest sign of that is a Polish study in which HbA1c tracked the weather closely in schoolchildren and showed no relationship at all in preschoolers from the same region — the difference between the two groups was how much their day changed in winter, not how cold it was. A weaker signal from New Delhi, where average HbA1c ran highest in the monsoon rather than the winter, points the same way, though that difference was not statistically significant. The one place cold acts directly is on a finger-prick reading, which runs falsely low when your hands are cold."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why does my HbA1c go up every winter?",
        "a": "Because almost everyone's does, by a small amount. In the largest type 1 diabetes dataset, covering 27,035 people, average HbA1c was 7.86% in September and 8.08% in January — about two tenths of a point. Smaller studies find swings of up to about 0.67 of a point. The usual drivers are less incidental walking once it is dark and wet, more comfort food, more winter infections, and less daylight. A February result that is a fraction higher than your September one is the most ordinary finding in seasonal diabetes research and is not a sign that something has gone wrong."
      },
      {
        "q": "Should I warm my hands before a finger-prick test?",
        "a": "Yes, and it is the single most actionable thing in this article. Cold constricts the small vessels in your fingers, blood moves through the tissue more slowly, and more of its glucose gets taken up before you prick — so the number comes out lower than the glucose actually circulating in you. In a documented case in a woman with Raynaud's, warming her limbs moved capillary readings from around 1.9 to 2.9 mmol/L up to around 4.6 to 5.6 mmol/L with nothing else changed. Use gloves, a warm drink or hand warmers. If you have reduced sensation in your hands, do not warm them on anything hot, because you will not feel it burning you."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can insulin freeze in a British winter?",
        "a": "It can, and frozen insulin should not be used, because freezing damages the protein and you can no longer rely on the dose. The realistic ways it happens here are a bag left in a car overnight, a pen carried in an outside coat pocket during a hard frost, and a cool bag packed with the ice block pressed straight against the insulin. Keep the pen you are using at room temperature, carry spares close to your body when you are out, and keep an ice block and a cartridge apart. Meters and pump handsets are also affected by cold and are better in an inside pocket than an outside one."
      }
    ]
